  "featureInfosGoogleAccountTitle": "Google account",
  "featureInfosGoogleAccountWhat": "The Google account on which the app creates playlists on YouTube.",
  "featureInfosGoogleAccountWhy": "You’re signed in once you’ve used the “Create on YouTube” option when sharing a playlist. The sign-in stays so that the next playlists don’t need the account chosen again.",
  "featureInfosGoogleAccountHow": "You sign in in a Google window (Google Play services), with permission only to manage your YouTube. The app remembers only the email address — it never sees the password or the token. The connection is used only when creating a playlist: the playlist is made as an unlisted copy.",
  "featureInfosGoogleAccountLibraries": "google_sign_in (Google Play services), YouTube Data API v3",
  "featureInfosGoogleAccountConnects": "accounts.google.com and googleapis.com — only at the moment a playlist is created.",
  "featureInfosListeningStatsTitle": "Listening stats",
  "featureInfosListeningStatsWhat": "A summary of what you listen to.",
  "featureInfosListeningStatsWhy": "Your most played songs, artists and albums, shares of your listening time, the week’s highlights and summary cards for the year and the seasons.",
  "featureInfosListeningStatsHow": "Every play is saved with how long you really listened to it. Everything is counted on the phone, in a local database.",
  "featureInfosListeningStatsLibraries": "sqflite (local database), custom charts",
  "featureInfosListeningStatsConnects": "When connected to a MusicDatabase server, plays sync with the account.",
  "featureInfosServerTitle": "MusicDatabase server",
  "featureInfosServerWhat": "The address of your MusicDatabase server — the program that keeps the song database and your music library.",
  "featureInfosServerWhy": "If it’s set up, the phone downloads the song database from the server (once a day), plays files from your library (the “Private server” source in Playback › Sources), sends correction requests and — once you’re signed in — syncs Library and the stats. Which of these it does, you choose in Sync — separately for the database, the library and database requests. Without a server the app still works: it downloads the database from GitHub, and the rest stays on the phone.",
  "featureInfosServerHow": "You can enter two addresses: a remote one (e.g. over Tailscale) and a home one, with the name of your home Wi-Fi network — at home the phone tries the home address first. Recognizing the Wi-Fi network needs location permission (an Android rule). “Use current connection” looks for the server on the network by itself. The phone downloads the database from the sources in the order set in Sync › Database (by default the server first, then GitHub). An empty database from the server (0 songs) doesn’t replace the one the phone already has without asking.",
  "featureInfosServerConnects": "The server addresses you entered; GitHub for the database (can be switched off, in the order you set).",
  "featureInfosOutboxTitle": "Correction requests",
  "featureInfosOutboxWhat": "An outbox of things the phone reports to MusicDatabase.",
  "featureInfosOutboxWhy": "The phone notices missing or wrong information: dead YouTube links (with a replacement video), your corrections of fields (the pencil in “Song info”), videos found for songs without a link, and played songs that aren’t in the database. The server reviews them and fixes the database — the fixes come back with the next database update.",
  "featureInfosOutboxHow": "Requests wait on the phone in small files. When the server is set up and switched on in Sync › Database requests, they’re sent by themselves (or with “Send now”). Without a server: “Send package…” packs them into a .zip file that you send through the “Share” options (e.g. with a messaging app), signed with your name. Until the server decides, the phone shows the value from the database, not yours.",
  "featureInfosOutboxLibraries": "sqflite (request files), archive (.zip package), share_plus (“Share”)",
  "featureInfosOutboxConnects": "The MusicDatabase server (PUT /inbox) — when it’s set up and switched on for requests. Package: nothing, it goes through the app chosen in “Share”.",
  "featureInfosNewPipePlaybackTitle": "NewPipeExtractor (playback)",
  "featureInfosNewPipePlaybackWhat": "The main way of playing audio from YouTube.",
  "featureInfosNewPipePlaybackWhy": "Lets you listen to songs from YouTube in the app: without video, ads or an account, with the screen off and in the background.",
  "featureInfosNewPipePlaybackHow": "NewPipeExtractor is the engine of the open-source NewPipe app. It asks YouTube for the video the way a browser does, reads the list of streams (when YouTube protects them, it runs YouTube’s player code itself to work out the address) and returns the address of the audio alone — Opus 160 kb/s or AAC 128 kb/s. The app’s player downloads it straight from YouTube’s video servers; played songs go into the cache. Android only.",
  "featureInfosNewPipePlaybackLibraries": "NewPipeExtractor (Java, GPLv3), just_audio (player)",
  "featureInfosNewPipePlaybackConnects": "youtube.com and YouTube’s video servers (googlevideo.com) — when a song from YouTube plays.",
  "featureInfosExplodePlaybackTitle": "youtube_explode (playback)",
  "featureInfosExplodePlaybackWhat": "A backup way of playing from YouTube — a library written in Dart.",
  "featureInfosExplodePlaybackWhy": "In case NewPipe stops working, or on devices without it.",
  "featureInfosExplodePlaybackHow": "It works similarly to NewPipe: it asks YouTube like a browser and looks for the audio address. Currently YouTube refuses its downloads (error 403).",
  "featureInfosExplodePlaybackConnects": "youtube.com and YouTube’s video servers.",
  "featureInfosNewPipeSearchTitle": "NewPipeExtractor (search)",
  "featureInfosNewPipeSearchWhat": "The main way of searching YouTube.",
  "featureInfosNewPipeSearchWhy": "Searching for songs and playlists on YouTube and matching database songs that have no video link.",
  "featureInfosNewPipeSearchHow": "The same library as for playback: it sends a query to YouTube like a browser and reads the results (videos, and playlists separately). No account and no key. YouTube is searched only when you clearly ask — with the online search button or when you tap a song that isn’t in any source — never on every letter. Android only.",
  "featureInfosNewPipeSearchConnects": "youtube.com — when searching.",
  "featureInfosExplodeSearchTitle": "youtube_explode (search)",
  "featureInfosExplodeSearchWhat": "A backup way of searching YouTube — a library in Dart.",
  "featureInfosExplodeSearchWhy": "When NewPipe fails, search still works (unlike playback, searching with this library works).",
  "featureInfosExplodeSearchHow": "It sends a query to YouTube like a browser and reads the results. No account and no key. Used only when a way higher up the list fails.",
  "featureInfosExplodeSearchConnects": "youtube.com — when searching.",
  "featureInfosYouTubeApiNote": "Work in progress — not yet tried in practice with a real key.",
  "featureInfosYouTubeApiWhat": "Google’s official YouTube search, with your key.",
  "featureInfosYouTubeApiWhy": "The most stable way of searching — it doesn’t break after changes to the YouTube website. Useful when NewPipe and youtube_explode fail.",
  "featureInfosYouTubeApiHow": "It needs a YouTube Data API key (free, from the Google Cloud Console — your own project with YouTube Data API v3 enabled), entered below in “YouTube API key”. The key stays only on the phone. Limit: 10,000 units a day per key, one search uses 100 — about 100 searches a day. Playback still goes through NewPipe.",
  "featureInfosYouTubeApiConnects": "www.googleapis.com — when searching, if the key is entered.",
  "featureInfosSpotifyApiNote": "Work in progress — not yet tried in practice with a real key.",
  "featureInfosSpotifyApiWhat": "Official Spotify playlist search, with the key of your Spotify app.",
  "featureInfosSpotifyApiWhy": "A more stable Spotify playlist search. Search also works without a key, but unofficially, and it may stop working.",
  "featureInfosSpotifyApiHow": "The key is the Client ID and Client Secret of an app you create for free on developer.spotify.com. The phone exchanges it for a short-lived token and searches through the Web API. Without a key (or when the API fails) it searches the way the player on open.spotify.com does without signing in. The key stays only on the phone. Importing playlists from links doesn’t use the API — it reads the playlist’s public page (the first 100 tracks).",
  "featureInfosSpotifyApiConnects": "accounts.spotify.com and api.spotify.com with a key; without a key open.spotify.com and api-partner.spotify.com — when searching for Spotify playlists.",
  "featureInfosAccountSyncTitle": "Account sync",
  "featureInfosAccountSyncWhat": "Exchanging your data between the phone and your account on the MusicDatabase server.",
  "featureInfosAccountSyncWhy": "It keeps your data the same on all your phones, and brings it back after a reinstall or on a new phone.",
  "featureInfosAccountSyncHow": "What syncs: playlists (with their picked covers), moods (and changes to global moods, if you have permission for that), Library folders, listening stats (every play), liked songs, songs and links saved for later. It works by itself — at start, when you come back to the app and a few seconds after a change — and ⟳ runs it right away. When the server doesn’t respond, changes wait on the phone. Playlists, moods and folders created before signing in are added to the account with the “… only on this phone” rows.",
  "featureInfosAccountSyncConnects": "The MusicDatabase server from Sync › Private server, on the account you’re signed in to.",
  "featureInfosHomeTitle": "Home",
  "featureInfosHomeWhat": "The home page: rows with your music and suggestions.",
  "featureInfosHomeWhy": "A quick way back to what you listen to, and hints about what to listen to next.",
  "featureInfosHomeHow": "Rows from Library (Recently played, Liked Songs, Your playlists, Your moods, Later) and recommendations worked out on the phone from your listening stats and the song database: your most played artists, albums you keep coming back to, new in the database, your genres and Forgotten (songs played at least twice, but not in the last 60 days). You can change the order and visibility of the rows in “Customize home page” (pinning, moving, hiding); the filters at the top narrow the view to songs, playlists or moods.",
  "featureInfosDiscoverTitle": "Discover",
  "featureInfosDiscoverWhat": "Browsing music one song at a time.",
  "featureInfosDiscoverWhy": "For discovering songs from the database you may not know.",
  "featureInfosDiscoverHow": "At the top you choose a category: the whole MusicDatabase database or one of the moods. Swiping up shows the next song. Nothing plays until you start it yourself: “Preview” plays the song from about a third of the way in, and “From the start” — the whole of it. With every song: save for later, add to playlist, share. Full albums (one recording) and hidden songs are skipped.",
  "featureInfosSongRadioTitle": "Song radio",
  "featureInfosSongRadioWhat": "A list of songs similar to the chosen one, played like a radio.",
  "featureInfosSongRadioWhy": "When you want to hear more with a similar vibe, without putting a playlist together.",
  "featureInfosSongRadioHow": "The phone picks up to 50 songs from the database most similar to the chosen one — a shared artist, genres (related ones too, e.g. “indie pop” and “pop”), moods, language, country and decade — with a little randomness. One artist takes at most 8 places, so the radio isn’t their discography. Full albums and hidden songs are skipped. MusicDatabase has no radio of its own, so this is an approximation worked out on the phone; for the same song the radio is always the same.",
